<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kandyan Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kandyan Convention was the agreement signed on 2 March 1815 between the British and the chiefs of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, after they had helped the British depose King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vikrama-rajasinha">Sri Vikrama Rajasinha</a>. It transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over Kandy to the British Crown, making the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> a British possession, while promising to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and the chiefs' rights. A great rebellion against British rule followed in 1817–1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kandyan-convention.jpg" alt="Kandyan Convention" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Government Archives of Sri Lanka/ Photographed by MediaJet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Kandyan_Convention_of_1815.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vikrama-rajasinha">Sri Vikrama Rajasinha</a>, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, was of South Indian ancestry and faced powerful opposition from the Sinhalese chiefs, who sought to limit his power. The chiefs organised a successful coup, and the king was imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a>. The convention is therefore unusual in that it was signed not by the monarch on the throne but by members of his court and other dignitaries of the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The treaty was signed in the Magul Maduwa, the Royal Audience Hall of the Royal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, by Sir Robert Brownrigg, the British governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, and the Kandyan chiefs. These included Molligoda the elder, first Adigar, Pilima Talawuwe the elder, second Adigar, and the dissawas of Uva, Matale, Walapane and other provinces; John D'Oyly, the government's chief translator, was among the witnesses. In the Sinhala version the signatories are the chiefs of Sihale. The authenticity of the native signatures has recently been questioned.</p><p>According to apocryphal accounts, a Buddhist monk of the kingdom, Wariyapola Sri Sumangala Thero, seized and trampled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Jack</a> hoisted by the British, demanding that the flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> be left flying until the convention was signed. The original copy of the convention is held in the archives of the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The proclamation had 12 clauses. The king, described as a Malabar (Tamil) king, forfeited all claims to the throne; he was declared deposed, the hereditary claim of his dynasty was abolished, and all his male relatives were banished. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> passed to the British sovereign, to be exercised through colonial governors, while the Adikarams, Disavas, Mohottalas and other subordinate officers kept the rights and powers of their ranks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was declared inviolable, and its rights were to be maintained and protected.</p><p>All physical torture and mutilation were abolished, and only the governor could pass a death sentence, with executions carried out in the presence of agents of the government. Justice was to follow the established customs of the country, though the government reserved the right to intervene. An earlier proclamation annexing the Three and Four Korales and Sabaragamuwa was repealed, revenues were to be collected for the King of the United Kingdom, and only the governor could facilitate trade.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kandyan Convention” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kandyan Convention</b> transferred sovereignty to the British Crown, and the kingdom became part of British Ceylon.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kandy">Kingdom of Kandy</a></li></ul></section>
